Relevant table & columns for the task  
(the session‑duration question can be answered with data that live only in THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS):

• EVENTS."browser"          : browser name to be evaluated; will be the final grouping column.  
• EVENTS."session_id"       : unique visitor session identifier; used to count sessions and to delimit the time span of one session.  
• EVENTS."user_id"          : lets you make sure one session really belongs to one user (if you want to enforce that rule).  
• EVENTS."created_at"       : event time‑stamp; MIN() and MAX() inside one "session_id" give the length of that session.  
• EVENTS."event_type" (optional) : could be used to exclude page‑views that are not part of the “real” browsing journey, but not strictly required.

Typical conditions that will be useful  
  – "browser"            IS NOT NULL  
  – "session_id"         IS NOT NULL  
  – HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "session_id") > 10   (to keep only browsers with > 10 sessions)  

Below are ten incremental Snowflake SQL snippets (each returns at most 20 rows) that you can run in order to explore the data all the way up to the final result.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotes and every query is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1. List a sample of distinct browser values that exist in the EVENTS table
SELECT DISTINCT "browser"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Count how many EVENTS rows each browser has (raw event count)
SELECT "browser",
       COUNT(*) AS "event_rows"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser"
ORDER BY "event_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count distinct sessions per browser
SELECT "browser",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "session_id") AS "sessions"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
  AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser"
ORDER BY "sessions" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Keep only browsers that have more than 10 sessions
SELECT "browser",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "session_id") AS "sessions_gt_10"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
  AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser"
HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "session_id") > 10
ORDER BY "sessions_gt_10" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Inspect min and max timestamps for a handful of sessions (pick one browser)
SELECT "browser",
       "session_id",
       MIN("created_at") AS "session_start",
       MAX("created_at") AS "session_end"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" = (SELECT DISTINCT "browser"
                   FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
                   WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
                   LIMIT 1)          -- any sample browser
  AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Compute raw session duration (micro‑seconds) for those same sessions
SELECT "browser",
       "session_id",
       (MAX("created_at") - MIN("created_at")) AS "session_duration_us"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" = (SELECT DISTINCT "browser"
                   FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
                   WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
                   LIMIT 1)
  AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Average session duration (still in micro‑seconds) for every browser
SELECT "browser",
       AVG(MAX("created_at") - MIN("created_at")) AS "avg_session_duration_us"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
  AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"          -- per session first …
QUALIFY 1 = 1                              -- Snowflake keeps aggregation scope tidy
-- Wrap the above in an outer query to average across sessions
LIMIT 0;   -- dummy; see next query for a cleaner two‑stage approach
```
(The previous step shows the logic but Snowflake needs a two‑stage aggregation; here is the clean version:)

```sql
-- 8. Stage‑1: build a helper view with one row per session & its duration
WITH session_durations AS (
    SELECT "browser",
           "session_id",
           (MAX("created_at") - MIN("created_at")) AS "session_duration_us"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
    WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
      AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"
)
SELECT *
FROM session_durations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Stage‑2: average duration per browser, keep only browsers with >10 sessions
WITH session_durations AS (
    SELECT "browser",
           "session_id",
           (MAX("created_at") - MIN("created_at")) AS "session_duration_us"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
    WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
      AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"
)
SELECT "browser",
       COUNT(*)                               AS "total_sessions",
       AVG("session_duration_us")             AS "avg_session_duration_us"
FROM session_durations
GROUP BY "browser"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
ORDER BY "avg_session_duration_us" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: the three browsers with the shortest average session duration
WITH session_durations AS (
    SELECT "browser",
           "session_id",
           (MAX("created_at") - MIN("created_at")) AS "session_duration_us"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."EVENTS"
    WHERE "browser" IS NOT NULL
      AND "session_id" IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY "browser", "session_id"
),
avg_duration AS (
    SELECT "browser",
           COUNT(*)                        AS "total_sessions",
           AVG("session_duration_us")      AS "avg_session_duration_us"
    FROM session_durations
    GROUP BY "browser"
    HAVING COUNT(*) > 10
)
SELECT "browser",
       "avg_session_duration_us"
FROM avg_duration
ORDER BY "avg_session_duration_us" ASC
LIMIT 3;
```

